296875, -0.03271484375, -0.0244140625, 0.008544921875, -0.0055999755859375, -0.0090179443359375, -0.004970550537109375, 0.01983642578125, -0.006038665771484375, 0.007694244384765625, -0.0079803466796875, 0.00684356689453125, 0.016204833984375, 0.03204345703125, -0.00081634521484375, -0.024017333984375, 0.0012054443359375, -0.0007295608520507812, 0.0292510986328125, -0.0101776123046875, 0.01385498046875, 0.0014257431030273438, -0.015655517578125, 0.0198822021484375, -0.00098419189453125, 0.00450897216796875, -0.01474761962890625, 0.030670166015625, 0.038970947265625, -0.01525115966796875, -0.0130767822265625, 0.005878448486328125, 0.00982666015625, 0.0122528076171875, 0.020843505859375, 0.007312774658203125, -0.0146331787109375, -0.005519866943359375, -0.01806640625, -0.00789642333984375, 0.00644683837890625, -0.0294189453125, 0.01439666748046875, -0.059051513671875, 0.0039520263671875, -0.018096923828125, -0.00591278076171875, 0.005619049072265625, -0.0273284912109375, 0.008453369140625, -0.0177154541015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents are systems that systematically enhance their problem-solving abilities by learning incrementally from successive streams of tasks. These agents persistently store and update natural language memories\u2014summaries and feedback after each episode\u2014which they can later consult to improve future performance. Such methods have demonstrated strong results across a variety of domains, including robotics, virtual assistants, and reasoning tasks, where adaptability and cumulative learning are crucial. However, while much attention has focused on mean performance, an important open question is the *reliability* of these agents: specifically, the *consistency* (similarity of performance across repeated runs with unchanged settings) and *robustness* (stability when facing different randomizations or task orderings) of their improvements.\n\nTo more precisely engage with these issues, we use the following operational concepts:\n- *Consistency* refers to minimal variation in measured outcomes when agents are run multiple times under identical configurations.\n- *Robustness* captures the maintenance of performance when external factors\u2014such as task ordering or stochastic variations\u2014change.\n- An *implicit curriculum* describes the unintentional pattern where easier tasks precede harder ones, as opposed to random or shuffled task sequences, often allowing for smoother progression (cf. Bengio et al., 2009).\n- *Memory drift* is the gradual accumulation of incorrect, ambiguous, or irrelevant information in memory, degrading future behavior; it is reflected as increasing occurrences of repeated mistakes or off-task actions over time.\n\nWe focus on three major research questions:\n1. To what extent do memory-based agents exhibit consistent performance across repeated runs or varied task orderings?\n2. What are the underlying drivers and patterns of fragility or instability in agents\u2019 improvements over time?\n3. Can interventions in memory/feedback structuring (e.g., via rubrics or more granular feedback) effectively reduce such weaknesses, and what limitations remain?\n\n**Experimental Setup and Environments**\n\nWe examine two established memory-based agent architectures, each differing in their approach to experience storage and retrieval:\n\n| Agent Type                        | Memory Content & Storage                                     | Memory Usage                                       |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| *Textual episodic memory agent*    | Stores natural-language summaries of actions, errors, corrections after each episode, appended sequentially to persistent memory. | Summaries are retrieved as a reference when facing new tasks; e.g., after an error, the agent consults past mistake summaries.      |\n| *RL agent with memory retrieval*   | Logs structured episode traces: state descriptions, actions, rewards, and feedback, all in text form.               | Prior to acting, retrieves top-k most similar past episodes (measured via embedding-based similarity with current task context), which inform action selection via a Transformer. |\n\nFor the *textual episodic memory agent* (see [Wang et al., 2023]), memory entries are formed from action logs and templated scripts, resulting in concise, human-readable summaries\u2014for example: \"Moved red block to blue square, but forgot to ring bell\u2014try ringing immediately after.\" In contrast, the *RL agent with memory retrieval* ([Lee et al., 2022]) stores more granular structural information for each episode, later retrieving those judged relevant through embedding distances.\n\nBoth agents use a 6-layer Transformer (512 hidden units, 8 attention heads, GELU activation). Hyperparameters were selected for stable training on initial tasks (Supplement provides details). We restrict the memory to a most-recent 100-entry sliding window to balance recency benefits and computational limits, minimizing overfitting to outdated experience.\n\n**Task Environments**\n\nWe test these agents on two environment types:\n- *Procedural tasks*: Agents operate in a 5\u00d75 grid-world and must follow instructions using a vocabulary of 50 verbs/objects (e.g., \"Move the green cup onto the table, then press the blue button after placing the red block\"). Complexity increases as tasks involve more subtasks or objects.\n- *Reasoning tasks*: Multi-step math or logic word problems such as, \"If you start with 4 coins, double them, give away 3, and add 2, how many left?\" Challenges are scaled by number/complexity of operations and distractor content.\n\nEach task is generated by randomizing from parameterized templates, ensuring a broad span of 3 difficulty levels (easy: single-step, medium: 2\u20133 steps, hard: 4+ steps). Vocabulary, operation types, and subtask counts vary widely to test agents\u2019 generalization abilities (see Supplement Table S1).\n\n**Evaluation Metrics and Statistical Analysis**\n\nOutcomes are measured with:\n(i) *Task completion rate*: percent of episodes achieving the task-specified goal for each environment/difficulty;\n(ii) *Average episode reward*: mean sum of all subgoal rewards per episode.\n\nWe run 200 episodes per agent and setting, across 10 random seeds (n=2000 per configuration). Task order is usually randomized (uniform sampling without replacement), unless curriculum comparisons specify fixed easy-to-hard ordering.\n\nStatistical comparisons (Welch\u2019s t-test, used due to potential non-normality and unequal variances) assess differences in completion/reward between orderings, agent types, and intervention vs. baseline. Improvements or declines are always referenced to the fixed-curriculum baseline; t-based confidence intervals and Cohen's d effect sizes are reported. P-values are exactly reported or <0.01 as appropriate (\u03b1=0.05, Holm-Bonferroni for multiple comparisons). Statistical methods, assumptions checks, and full test details are in Supplementary Materials.\n\nAll code, environments, and logging tools are provided in the Supplement.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Performance Consistency, Robustness, and Task Order Effects*\n\nAgents\u2019 completion rates show substantial variability depending on random initialization and especially on task order. For procedural tasks, completion rates range from 55% to 88% across seeds (mean 68%, SD 13.7%). For reasoning tasks, rates stretch from 41% to 73% (mean 56%, SD 11.2%). Performance across randomized task orders is consistently and significantly lower than for fixed easy-to-hard curricula (mean drop: 10\u201318% completion; curriculum baselines: 84% procedural/67% reasoning; shuffled means: 68%/56%; confidence interval [8.1\u201319.0%], p<0.01, Cohen's d>0.8). Roughly 30% of randomized runs show complete plateauing with no improvement, in sharp contrast to consistent upward trends under curriculum.\n\nWhen imposing adversarial or challenging task orders (hardest tasks early, or regular interleaving of distractors), performance degrades further\u2014average procedural completion drops to 60% (17\u201324% below curriculum baseline), and reasoning to 43%. These results remain significant after Holm-Bonferroni adjustments. Thus, even modest shifts in sequence can translate into large swings in agent learning.\n\nA crucial observation is the propagation of early memory errors. Because each episode\u2019s outcome is encoded into memory, an initial misunderstanding or mistake can snowball, guiding subsequent episodes astray. In 28% of procedural and 34% of reasoning runs, a major early misstep leads to repeated similar errors in later episodes. For instance, average episode reward SD increases by +4.5 immediately after the first significant memory error, indicating growing instability in learning trajectories.\n\n**Diagnosis of Memory Drift and Failure Modes**\n\nTo clarify sources of fragility, we manually analyzed 200 memory entries per experimental condition. In failed runs, 32% of entries were ambiguous, incomplete, or misleading, compared to only 9% in high-performing runs (>75% completion). Errors included incomplete subgoals (18%, e.g., \"stacked blocks\" omitting essential actions like \"rung bell\"), misattribution of failure causes (12%), and outdated or incorrect state/object references (7%). Most ambiguity stemmed from vague or insufficiently structured feedback.\n\nComparing before and after intervention (see Table 3), frequencies of ambiguous/incomplete entries fell from 32% to 18%, misattribution from 12% to 6%, and reference errors from 7% to 5%.\n\n**Interventions: Structured Rubrics and Granular Feedback**\n\nWe evaluated two main interventions to reduce ambiguity and error compounding:\n\n1. *Structured Rubrics*: After each episode, memory records are supplemented with explicit checklists detailing key subgoals. For example, after a grid-world episode, the entry includes: \"Red block placed on blue square? Yes/No. Bell rung? Yes/No.\" This enforces systematic documentation of what was completed or missed.\n\n2. *Programmatic Feedback*: The environment tags progress at the subtask level, marking each as pass/fail. Memories then enumerate which subgoals succeeded or failed instead of mere overall binary feedback; e.g., \"Placed green cup\u2014pass; pressed blue button\u2014fail,\" allowing finer-grained tracking.\n\nImplementation involved modifying templates for action/event logging, and designing environment wrappers to emit structured outcome reports.\n\nWith these interventions, worst-case completion rates improved from 55%\u219263% (procedural) and 41%\u219249% (reasoning), a mean 7% gain (95% CI [5\u20138%], p=0.016 paired t-test, Cohen\u2019s d=0.34). Standard deviations decreased by 2\u20134% across runs. The prevalence of ambiguous memory entries fell by 40%, with corresponding drops in incomplete/misattribution errors. However, overall run-to-run variance remained substantial (SD in the 14\u201319% range), indicating ongoing sensitivity to order and early mistakes.\n\n*Examples:* For a procedural task, a rubric-augmented memory might look like:  \n\"Actions taken: moved green cup, pressed red button. Checklist: Green cup on table? Yes. Bell rung? No\u2014forgot to ring after placing the cup.\"\n\n*Annotation/engineering cost*: Incorporating rubrics and structured feedback required"}}